20:47:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Software updates reach every device through a carefully orchestrated pipeline that combines developer releases, cloud-hosted distribution, and client-side checking. Here’s the typical journey: --- ### 1. The update leaves the developer’s hands - **Build & package**: Developers compile the new code and assets into a signed package. Many modern systems create **delta (binary diff) patches** — only the changed bytes between the old and new versions — to keep downloads small. - **Testing & staging**: The update passes through internal QA, then often a beta or “staged rollout” to a small percentage of real users. This catches last-minute issues before a global push. --- ### 2. Hosting and global distribution - **Upload to content servers**: The final package is uploaded to backend servers. For mobile apps, that’s Apple’s App Store or Google Play. For PC games, it’s Steam, Epic Games Store, or the publisher’s own CDN. For consoles, it’s Sony, Microsoft, or Nintendo. - **Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)**: To handle millions of simultaneous downloads, the update files are cached on thousands of edge servers around the world. When a device requests the update, it’s served from a nearby CDN node, keeping speeds high and load off the central server. - **Metadata / version manifest**: The publisher updates a small manifest file (e.g., version number, file checksums, download URLs). This is what devices actually read to discover that an update exists. --- ### 3. How each device learns about the update There are two common wake-up mechanisms, often used together: - **Pull (polling)**: The device periodically checks the server for a new version — when the app is launched, during a system background task, or on a fixed schedule (e.g., Steam checks at startup). It downloads the manifest, compares with its current version, and triggers a download if needed. - **Push notifications**: Some platforms (iOS, Android, modern game launchers) send a lightweight push notification waking the device/app and telling it “a new update is available.” The device then polls in earnest. Push alone doesn’t deliver the update; it just signals the device to start the download. --- ### 4. Download and installation on the device - **Download the patch or full build**: If delta patching is supported, the client downloads only the diff and then reassembles the full new version locally. Otherwise, it downloads the complete installer. - **Integrity check**: The downloaded files are verified using cryptographic hashes (checksums, code signatures). This ensures the update wasn’t corrupted in transit and hasn’t been tampered with. - **Apply & commit**: The client unpacks the new files, overwrites or merges existing ones, and often writes a record of the new version. Many systems apply the update in a “staged” manner — first to a temporary location, then atomically switch over — so a crash or power loss doesn’t leave the software broken. - **Post-update cleanup**: Old, unused files may be removed, and caches rebuilt. The device is now running the new version. --- ### 5. Why every device gets it (and exceptions) - The update is **universally available** the moment the publisher flips the release switch — the same files on the CDN serve all devices of the same platform and region. - **Gradual rollouts** can limit the percentage of devices that see the update at first, but eventually it’s made available to 100%. - Devices that are offline, out of storage, or lack permission won’t receive it until the conditions are met, but the infrastructure is designed to serve them the update as soon as they check in. In essence, an update travels from a single source of truth through a massive, scalable network, and every device armed with the right authentication and a network connection can pull it down — seamlessly and securely.
